; A NEGATIVE VALUE IN A NON-NEGATIVE RANGE ABOVE THE WORD TRAPS, EVERYWHERE
; (ADR 0029).
;
; `(int 0 (pow 2 200))` denotes [0, 2^201). `d` is `a - b`, negative once b
; passes a, and the answer `d + b` is `a` again, so the only thing wrong is the
; intermediate: outside the declared range, and caught at the operation that
; produced it.
;
; Before ADR 0029 the fixed limbs trapped (the final borrow of a subtraction) and
; JavaScript trapped (its shift floors a negative to -1n), while Go's `BitLen`
; and Java's `bitLength` read a magnitude and printed a. Whether a program
; answered depended on where it ran and how its integers were stored.
;
; skip: windows
;
; windows is skipped for printing, as big-subdiv is: fixed limbs have no
; decimal conversion there yet.
;
; big-repr: both

; inputs: 0 5 6
; prints: string
; expect: 5000000000000000000000000000000 5000000000000000000000000000000 trap

(export calc)

(sig calc ((n (int 0 6))) (int 0 (pow 2 200)))

(def calc (n)
  (let a 5000000000000000000000000000000
       b (* 1000000000000000000000000000000 n)
       d (- a b)
    (+ d b)))

(def run (n) (big-str (calc n)))
